variant_source_before.chapters/08-4-directions.tex:
\subsection{Risk-controlled agreement with a reuse-aware objective}

A future cost-aware policy needs valid query-level agreement bounds, matched-regime counts, a fixed reference, sampling/order assumptions and drift-triggered renewal. Absolute GT targets additionally require labels or a justified reference-to-GT link.

Compare fixed sampling, reusable rank curves and cost-aware choice on held-out pools using total time/tail agreement. A four-cell sequential controller lost to extra launches. Success requires lower total cost than a quality-matched control while meeting the risk bound.

\subsection{Capacity-aware residual placement}

A future residual cache could retain primary codes on-device and fetch missing residual blocks from host memory. Fix codes, routing, survivors and arithmetic; vary device cap, batch and locality. Measure bytes, hits, overlap, tail latency and QPS. Lowering $\alpha$ alone does not extend this storage capacity.

variant_source_before.chapters/03-4-frontiers.tex:
The competing families cover graph traversal (CAGRA fp32/int8),
product-quantised IVF and GPU IVF-RaBitQ \cite{cagra,pq,ivfrabitq}.
FP32 GPU brute force provides an exhaustive-search reference. Each family
sweeps its own implementation parameters (\cref{tab:3-grids}); the recorded
index configurations can differ in storage and reconstruction fidelity.
Consequently the main frontier measures achievable speed at a quality
target within these grids. A separate memory account accompanies it.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\captionof{table}[Principal E1 tuning dimensions]{Principal E1 tuning dimensions. Exact per-point parameters and
coverage extensions remain in the raw records. The grid is finite and
differs between methods.}
\label{tab:3-grids}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.22\linewidth}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
Family & Recorded search dimensions \\
\midrule
JHQ & Probes and $\alpha$; initial probes 64/128/256 and
$0.5/1/2$ times the workload budget; coverage extensions reach probe
1,024 and larger/smaller budgets \\
CAGRA fp32/int8 & Graph degree 32/64; search width 1/2/4/8;
intermediate top-$k$ capacity 32--512 \\
IVF-PQ & Eight-bit subcodes; 192/384/768-byte codes at $d=768$,
384/768/1,536 at higher dimensions; fp16/fp32 lookup settings;
probes 16--512 \\
IVF-RaBitQ & 4/8/9 bits per dimension, supported search modes,
probes 16--1,024; additional 4,096-list OpenAI indexes \\
GPU brute force & Exhaustive FP32 squared-L2 search, common host boundary \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Dominance and the measured frontier are
\[
 a\succ b\iff R_a\geq R_b,\ Q_a\geq Q_b,\
 (R_a,Q_a)\ne(R_b,Q_b).
\]
\begin{equation}
Q_{\rm meas}(r)=\max\{Q_i:R_i\geq r\}.
\label{eq:3-measured-frontier}
\end{equation}
No qualifying measured point means undefined. For adjacent frontier points,
\begin{equation}
\log Q_{\rm int}(r)=(1-\lambda)\log Q_a+\lambda\log Q_b,
\qquad \lambda=\frac{r-R_a}{R_b-R_a}.
\label{eq:3-interpolated-frontier}
\end{equation}
The parser snaps within $5\times10^{-4}$, otherwise interpolates without extrapolation. T5 retains qualifying measured endpoints below a method's minimum recall. Both estimators are reported near ties; storage is unmatched.

Frontiers exclude failed/invalid outputs, not repeat-ID flags alone. Isolated reruns replace contaminated builds; skipped Stella batch-1,000 files add no points (\cref{sec:a-e1-protocol}). Int8 CAGRA remains included (\cref{fig:7-frontiers}); older ADC/poster plots keep their own scope.

