\subsection{Mailman-style shared sums: arithmetic reuse meets scatter cost}
\label{sec:8-mailman}

Mailman reuses finite-alphabet matrix--vector sums \cite{mailman}. We test atomic buckets, private histograms and widths 4/6/8 against packed two-table scoring on frozen Vogue/arXiv/OpenAI-3072 streams, 128 probes, batches 8/1,000. Codes and mathematical scores stay fixed.

The best alternatives reach only 0.187--0.581 of baseline throughput (\cref{fig:8-negative}). Bucket accumulation dominates profiles. List bias repeats destinations; private histograms need more state/shared updates. Narrower groups sometimes help, but no tested variant wins. We stopped these implementations, leaving other reuse designs open.

The pilot emits scores, so FP32/FP64 cutoff differences do not establish neighbour-pipeline equivalence. For scorer ratio $s$ and replaceable fraction $f$,
\[
 T'/T=(1-f)+f/s.
\]
This is a replacement model, not an end-to-end measurement: integration stopped. The dictionary study stopped at structural statistics; neither contributes a system-frontier point.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ch8/negative-evidence.pdf}
\captionof{figure}[Mailman and rank-oriented coding]{Two distinct evidence boundaries. Left: best measured
Mailman scorer / packed-table scorer speed on an RTX 5090, with Vogue,
arXiv and OpenAI-3072 at two batches and 128 probes; all ratios are below
one. Right: minimum survivor
counts meeting the original JHQ recall target on arXiv's 400-query FP64
offline test split. The candidate reduction is not a GPU speedup.}
\label{fig:8-negative}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

\subsection{Rank-oriented coding: a local gain is not a general replacement}
\label{sec:8-rank}

The ranking replay uses FP64 exported candidates and 400/200/400 calibration/\allowbreak{}validation/\allowbreak{}test queries. It tests weighted scores, median/list thresholds, learned losses and linear-MMSE decoding. Lower reconstruction/inner-product MSE can worsen neighbour order; changed primary levels also move residual reference points.

ArXiv median thresholds reduce the minimum tested qualifying count from 875 to 375; two other workloads fail the criterion. Rotation preserves corpus-mean norm, leaving bias despite Gaussian intuition. Centring must also preserve the refined-score ceiling; this gain is workload-specific.

A GPU follow-up imports median thresholds/levels and retrains residuals. At arXiv's original $\alpha=100$ target it gains $1.40/1.21\times$ at batches 8/1,000. Vogue reaches only 0.9630 versus target 0.9644; OpenAI-3072 ties. Three-round results use separate states/queries outside E1.

variant_source_before.chapters/06-3-search.tex:
\subsection{Bisection and complete enumeration}

Write $J=|A|$. The implementation computes the sample reference once,
then searches for the largest acceptable index in the descending grid:
\par\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0.15em]
\item Set $l=0$, $h=J-1$. Index zero is the reference fallback.
\item While $l<h$, test $j=\lfloor(l+h+1)/2\rfloor$ by running
$A[j]$ and computing $D_S(A[j])$.
\item If $D_S(A[j])\leq\epsilon$, set $l=j$; otherwise set $h=j-1$.
\item Return $A[l]$. A caller applies it before processing reuse batches.
\end{enumerate}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip
The eight-point grid needs at most three complete candidate searches
in addition to the reference. Invocation, host-comparison complexity and
timing boundaries are specified in \cref{sec:a-budget-contract}.

Enumeration reruns the reference and all seven budgets on the same sample. Both timers include a fresh reference. Enumeration handles acceptance holes; bisection matches its minimum only when accepted indices form a prefix.

\subsection{A sufficient condition for monotonicity}

\par\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{proposition}[Nested survivors preserve reference membership]
\label{prop:6-monotonicity}
For one query, assume fixed routing and survivor sets
$C_\alpha\subseteq C_\beta\subseteq C_{\alpha_{\rm ref}}$ whenever
$\alpha\leq\beta\leq\alpha_{\rm ref}$. Assume at least $k$ valid
survivors, fixed refined scores and one deterministic total order for
final ties. Each result is the exact top-$k$ in that order. Then
\begin{equation}
 |\mathcal R_\alpha\cap\mathcal R_{\rm ref}|
 =|C_\alpha\cap\mathcal R_{\rm ref}|,
 \qquad D_S(\alpha)\geq D_S(\beta).
 \label{eq:6-monotonicity}
\end{equation}
The latter statement holds when these conditions hold for every sampled
query.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $x\in\mathcal R_{\rm ref}\cap C_\alpha$, the common total order gives
\begin{equation*}
 |\{y\in C_\alpha:y\prec x\}|
 \leq|\{y\in C_{\alpha_{\rm ref}}:y\prec x\}|<k.
\end{equation*}
Hence $x\in\mathcal R_\alpha$. Together with
$\mathcal R_\alpha\subseteq C_\alpha$, this proves the intersection
identity in \cref{eq:6-monotonicity}. Nestedness gives
$|C_\alpha\cap\mathcal R_{\rm ref}|
 \leq|C_\beta\cap\mathcal R_{\rm ref}|$.
Summing missing reference counts over queries proves
$D_S(\alpha)\geq D_S(\beta)$.
\end{proof}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Fixed-key exact primary top-$c$ gives nesting; fixed-key refinement then gives monotone agreement. This supports bisection, without making GT recall or runtime monotone as the pool grows.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\input{figures/ch6/nesting-example}
\caption[Nested and non-nested candidate pools]{Constructed $k=1$ example with refined order $a\prec b\prec c$
and reference $\{a\}$. The nested case preserves reference membership.
With all primary scores tied, different tie choices can produce the
non-nested middle pool and break monotonicity. These are explanatory
sets, not measured GPU outputs.}
\label{fig:6-nesting}
\end{figure}

\subsection{A corollary for the evaluated grouped path}

Within a draw, $S$, routing and scan threads are fixed; \texttt{scheduled\_groups(S)} ignores $c$. Grouped budgets use the same LUT sums. Fixed $B_r$, 16 lanes and final $(\refineddist,\mathrm{ID})$ order keep candidate scores fixed.

\par\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{corollary}[Grouped calibration]
\label[corollary]{cor:6-grouped-calibration}
For the evaluated grouped configuration, under the representation and
shared-bound contracts of Chapters 4 and 5, primary survivors are nested
across budgets. Consequently \Cref{prop:6-monotonicity} applies and
bisection returns the same minimum accepted grid budget as enumeration.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
\Cref{lem:5-buffer,prop:5-local-coverage,prop:5-shared-bound} give exact
primary top-$c$ under the common key $(\primarydist,\mathrm{position})$.
Its prefixes nest as $c$ grows. The fixed per-candidate refinement and
exact final total order supply the remaining premises of
\Cref{prop:6-monotonicity}.
\end{proof}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

The corollary covers 2,176/2,304 draws; 128 OpenAI-3072 draws at $S=128$ use distance-only selection. Every recorded curve is monotone and matches enumeration, a finite check (\cref{sec:7-budget-trace}).

\paragraph{Full-pool coverage.}
Two FULL cells group Vogue; ten use $\gscan=0$. All twelve match enumeration. The ten distance-only cells lie outside \cref{cor:6-grouped-calibration}; general nesting needs a common cutoff order or no tie crossing grid/reference cutoffs. Sharing the held-out path supplies neither condition.

\paragraph{A rank-based extension.}
Under the same nesting/fixed-score premises, one-based primary ranks give
\begin{equation}
 D_S(\alpha)=\sum_{q\in\mathcal Q_S}\sum_{x\in\mathcal R_{\rm ref}(q)}
 \mathbf1\{\operatorname{rank}_{\rm P}(x)>c(\alpha)\}.
 \label{eq:6-rank-curve}
\end{equation}
A reference-rank export could recover all grid counts from one search. The harness instead reruns budgets; rank extraction remains future work.

